Chapter 45 – Part 1
Curtis lay back on the couch.
He draped an arm over his head, braid sliding off the cushion, and yawned like the month had finally found his bones.
“Guess the lack of sleep is catching up again.”
Mika didn’t ask permission.
She crawled up and lay on his chest, ear over the compression shirt, one hand tucked against his side like the kitchen floor had used up her bravery and this was what was left.
“I would be tired too, if i went through what have.”
His arm came down around her without thinking about counts. Not a kiss. Not a door. Just weight. Her hair smelled like outside and someone else’s building. His heartbeat was still too loud from the fee.
“Insomnia’s an old roommate,” he muttered at the ceiling. “It doesn’t care if I cooked. Or bled. Or told the truth.”
“Then shut up and be caught up.” Mika’s voice was already going soft. “I’m not leaving yet. Namie’s still muted. Seiji still has the room. You still have a couch.”
Curtis huffed once, barely a laugh.
The oni stayed on the table with its lights off.
Afternoon pressed against the windows of a furnished flat that had seen a dullahan at dawn and a girl at lunch. His eyes dragged shut under his own wrist.
Mika rose and fell with his breathing.
For a little while Ikebukuro could keep its riots and its lists.
A boy who did not sleep and a girl who should have been in a locked room slept anyway, stacked on the same couch, the month quiet between them.
Mika’s face found his.
Sleep had not quite taken her. Her mouth brushed close, then her hand slid up under the compression shirt, palm to warm skin, and kept going until her fingers found the waistband.
Curtis caught her wrist.
Not hard. Just there.
“Mika. I’m not denying you. But think this through before you cross this line.” His voice was rough from the yawn and the fee and her weight on his chest. “I don’t mind. But I don’t want you to regret it. Or hold anything.”
She went still.
Then she smiled, small and gentler than the girl who collected kisses.
“You are truly a gentleman.” She drew her hand back, slow, and rested it on the outside of his shirt instead. “Okay. I will think this through. And then maybe next time.”
He let her wrist go.
His arm stayed around her.
“Next time isn’t a promise I collect,” he said. “It’s yours if you still want it when this afternoon isn’t sitting on a month and a kitchen floor.”
Mika nodded against him.
“Seiji has the room. You have the couch. I’m not using either of you to not think.” A quieter breath. “Thank you for stopping me like a person.”
“Thank you for letting me.”
The oni kept its dead eyes on the ceiling.
They stayed stacked on the couch, line uncrossed, two kisses still two, and Mika Harima chose to think instead of take. Curtis Nightstar closed his eyes with her hand over his heart instead of under it, and for once the gentleman part of him and the tired part of him agreed.
Justice leaned on the window that was not a window.
He watched his brother on the couch with a living girl on his chest and an oni on the table, and he grinned like the month had never happened.
“Aw. You wuss.”
Curtis did not hear him. Mika did not hear him. The city did not hear him.
Justice hooked his thumbs in his pockets anyway, talking to a room that had already chosen kindness.
“You’ll have sex with a headless woman but say no to a college girl your own age. You’re fuckin’ weird sometimes, bro. Hahahah.”
The laugh was fond. It always had been, even when it was cruel.
“Gentleman act. ‘Think it through.’ You bled on the floor for her and then you filed the waistband under later. I would’ve already made it a story.” He tipped his head, watching Mika’s hand rest polite on the shirt. “You’re not me. I know. That’s the point. Still. A month in and you’re collecting almosts. Celty. This one. The quiet girl at school. You’re going to trip over your own door if you keep holding it open and never walking through.”
On the couch, Curtis’s breathing had gone even.
Justice’s smile thinned at the edges.
“Sleep, you idiot. I’ll keep the watch. You already did the party trick. Don’t add regret to the tile.”
He stayed at the glass.
Ikebukuro moved under him.
A dead boy teased a living one for being kinder than the joke, and for once the punchline did not need an audience.
Mika waited until his arm went heavy.
Then she kissed him.
It was quiet. No count said out loud. Just her mouth on his sleeping one, careful of the gauze, like she could leave the words there if she did not make him answer.
She slipped off the couch, found her shoes, and paused at the door with her palm on the frame.
“Thank you, Curtis-kun. For everything.”
A breath.
“And I love you.”
The latch closed softer than a mute.
Justice grinned at the couch like the month had handed him a present.
“Oooooh. She said it.”
Curtis did not move. The kiss sat on his mouth uncollected. The sentence sat in the hall already gone.
Justice leaned over him, spirit-hands useless.
“Hey. Are you awake? Hey. Wake up.” He snapped fingers that made no sound. “I hate that no one can hear me sometimes.”
The oni stared.
The juice bottle sweated on the table.
Justice straightened, still smiling, a little mean with it.
“College girl said the big one and you slept through it, you gentleman idiot. Celty got a night. This one left a confession on a dead phone line. You’re gonna wake up and think she just went home.”
He looked at the door Mika had used.
“I heard you, kid. He didn’t. That’s the joke.”
Curtis Nightstar slept with a month on his chest and three words he had not earned the chance to answer.
Justice kept talking to a brother who could not hear him, because that was the only job left.